## Issue 1: The 31-Second SQL Generator Call

Your instinct is right — this has nothing to do with the loading-status feature. The status feature only *displays* what's happening; it doesn't change execution time. Look at the actual numbers from your own log:

```
Intent LLM took 5.86 seconds
SQL Gen LLM took 31.05 seconds     ← the outlier
Chart LLM took 5.00 seconds
```

Three calls to the same LLM gateway, wildly different durations, and it's not proportional to prompt length in any way that would fully explain a 5x-6x jump. This points to one of three causes, and you can distinguish between them with a five-minute test rather than guessing.

**Most likely cause — this specific prompt structure is expensive for your gateway, not just "long."** Look at what's different about the SQL generator prompt versus the other two: it's the only one of the three that includes a large, structurally repetitive block — `schema_block`, which is the full table/column schema description built by `build_schema_description()`. Depending on how many tables and columns RMS_ANALYTICS currently has registered, that block could be genuinely large, and more importantly, it's the kind of dense, enumerated, rule-heavy content that tends to make LLMs generate more deliberated (slower) completions, especially combined with the 10 numbered "STRICT RULES" sitting right below it. The intent classifier prompt, by contrast, is short and asks for a two-word JSON answer. The chart prompt sends only the top 100 rows of already-filtered data, not the full schema.

**Second possible cause — gateway-side queueing/model routing, not prompt content at all.** If your internal LLM gateway load-balances across multiple backend instances or has per-request-type routing, it's possible the SQL generation call specifically is landing on a slower path for reasons unrelated to your prompt. You mentioned earlier in this project that your gateway has known quirks (stripping tool_calls, embedding API 401s) — it's plausible it has inconsistent latency characteristics too.

**Third, less likely but worth ruling out — output length.** If the LLM is asked to produce a long, deliberated single-block SQL query with no delimiter to stop early, and it's "reasoning" through the 10 rules before emitting the query (some models do visible or invisible step-by-step deliberation even without an explicit chain-of-thought instruction), that adds real wall-clock time independent of gateway load.

### How to actually diagnose this instead of guessing

Ask your AI to add one more timing checkpoint: log the **exact byte/token length of the schema_block** being sent for this specific query, alongside the existing timing print. Then run the same query type against two or three different collections with meaningfully different schema sizes (if you have RMS_ANALYTICS and HRMS_ANALYTICS both live, compare their generation times for a similarly-phrased question). If HRMS (smaller schema, fewer tables per your earlier schema registry work) generates noticeably faster than RMS for a comparable question, that confirms schema size as the driver, and the fix is straightforward: **trim the schema block to only the tables relevant to the question**, rather than always sending every table registered for that collection. You already have the infrastructure for this — a cheap pre-filter step (keyword match on table purpose descriptions, or even just always including the fact table and letting the LLM ask via a second turn if it needs more) would shrink the prompt substantially for most single-table questions.

If timing stays roughly flat regardless of schema size, the bottleneck is on the gateway side, not your prompt — and the fix becomes a conversation with whoever operates that internal gateway about routing/capacity for this endpoint, not something you can solve by editing prompts.

**One thing NOT to do:** don't strip down the 10 numbered rules to "shorten the prompt" as a first attempt. Those rules are there because you added them one at a time in response to real bugs you hit earlier in this build — the FACT/DIMENSION join rule specifically fixed a real data-integrity bug you found. Cutting rules to save latency risks reintroducing bugs you already paid to fix. Diagnose the actual cause first; don't trade correctness for speed on a guess.

